module gunbc.roadmap_sizing

import std.types { String, Bool, NonEmptyStr }

// SIZE IS DERIVED, NEVER AUTHORED. Until this module existed, every roadmap work item carried a
// hand-authored intricacy/volume pair inside the NodeSizing coproduct, chosen by whoever wrote the
// row before any of the work was done. That is an estimate wearing a fact's clothes: nothing
// observed it, nothing could contradict it, and two rows sharing a cell shared only their authors'
// moods. Consumers then read it as though it were measured -- the dispatch request published it to
// the spawner, the presentation badged it, and the forecast keyed its cycle-time calibration
// population on it, so a guess entered a schedule as evidence.
//
// THE SUBJECT IS A TASK, NOT A ROADMAP NODE. This module imports nothing from the roadmap: a task
// worth sizing may be a plan item in another repository, a dashboard work item, or a brief that
// never becomes a RoadmapNode at all, and a deriver that took a RoadmapNode would make the roadmap
// the only sizable thing in the corpus. SizingSubject is the minimum a sizing process reads:
// WHAT the work is (title), WHAT IT INVOLVES (description -- the decision surface intricacy comes
// from and the extent of material volume comes from), and WHAT DONE MEANS (exit_criterion, absent
// where the subject genuinely declares none, never an empty string standing in for one). It
// deliberately does not carry the repository, the acceptance route, the owner or the dependency
// edges: none of those change how hard or how large the work is, and a deriver that could see them
// could accidentally size by lane. The signature is wider than today's stub needs, and that is
// deliberate -- the stub is the seam, and a seam shaped around the one caller that exists today
// would have to be recut by the first caller that is not a roadmap node.
//
// THE DERIVER IS A STUB AND SAYS SO. derive_task_sizing is the single home for the answer, and
// today it refuses with a typed, located refusal rather than returning a fabricated cell. SizingNotDerivable is
// a DISTINCT ARM from SizingDerived, so no consumer can read a stub answer as a derived one: a
// default returned through the derived arm would be the fabricated plausible output DESIGN section
// 5 forbids, and it would be indistinguishable from an answer the deriver stood behind.
//
// WHAT THE CENSUS ACTUALLY FOUND, stated without the phrase it used to carry. No dispatch, launch,
// admission, or provider-selection decision depended on intricacy or volume. The values had three
// live uses: the spawn artifact rendered them, the roadmap presentation rendered them, and the
// forecast used both their presence and their VALUES -- via cell equality -- to select calibration
// populations and determine whether a projection could be produced. The rendering losses are
// explicit output changes; the forecast capability loss is the declared section 4b(3) drop.
//
// An earlier draft of this note said no consumer's control flow ever branched on intricacy or
// volume. That was FALSE and it was load-bearing: roadmap_forecast calibrate_cell selects its
// sample with filter(o => sizing_cell_eq(...)), which compares intricacy and volume, so the values
// decided membership in the calibration population. The sentence is recorded here as retracted
// rather than deleted, because it was the sentence licensing "refusal is safe because the census
// says so" and a reader who met only the corrected version would not know that claim had rested on
// something untrue.
//
// WHAT THE DERIVER CONSUMES IS THE SUBJECT, AND NOTHING ELSE. derive_task_sizing takes a
// SizingSubject: identity, title, description, exit criterion. An earlier draft said the deriver
// would consume gunbc.roadmap_forecast's calibrated per-node work and dependency-closure facts;
// that is a DIFFERENT question and the confusion mattered. roadmap_forecast's own note calls
// remaining-cost-to-complete a FOLLOW-ON DERIVED READ and says in terms that it is "never an
// authored field on RoadmapNode or NodeSizing" -- so closure cost is computed FROM sizes and
// cannot also be an input to them without the circle the note refuses.
//
// SO THIS IS A DERIVED ESTIMATE, NOT AN OBSERVED FACT, and the vocabulary has to say so. Reading a
// task's title, description and exit criterion and answering how hard the work is and how much of
// it there is, is a judgement about work NOBODY HAS PERFORMED YET. There is no observation to
// ground it, because the thing it describes has not happened. Calling the result a measurement or a
// size fact would be asserting as deduced what is only inferred (DESIGN section 4d), which is
// precisely the defect the authored cells had: an author's ordinal guess that nothing observed and
// nothing could contradict. Deriving the guess does not make it an observation; it makes it an
// estimate with a stated method, which is a different and honest thing.

type SizingSubjectId = NonEmptyStr where brand("SizingSubjectId")

type SizingSubject {
  subject: SizingSubjectId
  title: String
  description: String
  exit_criterion: String?
}

type Intricacy
  = IntricacyLow
  | IntricacyMedium
  | IntricacyHigh

type Volume
  = VolumeSmall
  | VolumeMedium
  | VolumeLarge

// NodeSizing is now a RECORD carrying only size. The name is kept because the concept is the one
// the coproduct used to hide; what left is everything that was never a size -- the repository and
// the acceptance route, which are now gunbc.roadmap_model WorkItemDispatch, and the
// is-this-dispatchable-at-all discriminant, which was never sizing either.
type NodeSizing {
  intricacy: Intricacy
  volume: Volume
}

// WHY A REFUSAL CARRIES ITS SUBJECT AND NOT A CAUSE COPRODUCT, stated because the first shape here
// was one. There is exactly one reason a derivation refuses today -- the deriver is not built --
// and a coproduct with one arm is a type alias in this substrate, not a closed set. Rather than
// mint a second arm nobody measured just to make the coproduct legal, the arm itself is the typed
// refusal and the reason is one declared sentence below. A partially built deriver that can fail
// two distinguishable ways introduces the coproduct then, with both arms real.
data sizing_deriver_unbuilt_detail: String = "sizing is derived from the task, and the deriver is not built yet; the authored intricacy/volume fields it replaces were deleted rather than left standing as a second authority"

type NodeSizingDerivation
  = SizingDerived { subject: SizingSubjectId, sizing: NodeSizing }
  | SizingNotDerivable { subject: SizingSubjectId, detail: String }

fn derive_task_sizing(subject: SizingSubject) -> NodeSizingDerivation {
  SizingNotDerivable {
    subject: subject.subject,
    detail: sizing_deriver_unbuilt_detail,
  }
}

fn intricacy_label(i: Intricacy) -> String {
  match i {
    IntricacyLow => "low"
    IntricacyMedium => "medium"
    IntricacyHigh => "high"
  }
}

fn volume_label(v: Volume) -> String {
  match v {
    VolumeSmall => "small"
    VolumeMedium => "medium"
    VolumeLarge => "large"
  }
}

fn node_sizing_eq(a: NodeSizing, b: NodeSizing) -> Bool {
  a.intricacy == b.intricacy && a.volume == b.volume
}
